v2.reason:
(a) The code does what the finding says. dispatch_inner (engine.rs:953-980) takes store.lock() and opens the transaction before it calls any handler registered with `register`, and runs a staged op's finish under that same lock. WorktreeRemove (git.rs:129), BranchCreate (345), BranchDelete (445), Stage (490), Unstage (507) and CleanMerged (693) are all plain `register`. Each one reaches worktree::git_mutate, which is a bare `Command::output()` with no timeout (worktree.rs:26-36). CommitOp's finish runs `git commit --no-verify` inside the transaction (571). guardrail.confirm is plain `register` (guardrail.rs:339), and through replay_registered/invoke_registered (engine.rs:328-351) it runs git.commit's whole prepare inside the open transaction: `git add -A`, refresh_git, run_user_pre_commit (bare `.output()`, hooks.rs:210) and staged_numstat. D149 lists the ops it converted to staged, and none of these six is on that list.

(b) Who reaches it. The desktop Code page calls git.stage and git.unstage (per file, and "Stage all" / "Unstage all"), git.commit (a smart commit sends all=true when nothing is staged), git.branch.create (checkout:true, optional start point), git.branch.delete and guardrail.confirm (code_git.rs:280/392/474/1035/1136/1750). Agents can call stage, unstage and commit over MCP. Every call holds the lock. On the perf fixture of 1,500 files the cost was 4-30 ms (BASELINE §, report.md). Multi-second holds come from:
- `git add` hashing and compressing large binary or LFS assets;
- `git reset HEAD -- paths`, which refreshes the whole index. git itself warns when that passes 2 s.
- `git switch -c` to a start point that differs from HEAD in a large repo, with smudge filters;
- merged_tips walking the base's entire history whenever any branch is unmerged;
- a commit-signing prompt, which waits until the user answers;
- the replay path.

The replay path is not rare. refuse_or_user_hold holds every user commit that goes over the caps (40 files or 2000 lines by default, settings.rs:29) or touches a protected path. So the replay runs exactly on the biggest commits, where staged_numstat (a gix::open, a full index parse and a line diff per file), `git status` and any user pre-commit hook cost the most.

(c) What happens. Every request that needs the store waits: all mutations, the reads inside unlocked queries, and every agent request, because the socket door's token check takes the lock (engine.rs:1377-1384). That last one means every agent's PreToolUse guardrail.gate call for Bash, Edit or Write stalls, along with every MCP call. Keystrokes are spared (D148). Nothing is lost. The wait is unbounded only when a child never exits, since there is no timeout, or when a signing prompt is left unanswered.

(d) High fits the rubric's "a freeze of the bus" in normal Code-page flows on repos with large assets or indexes, which is this app's Unreal/Blender audience. On small code repos the holds are milliseconds.

(e) For the six `register` ops the fix is right: use the BranchSwitch pattern (register_staged, git work in prepare, output_with_timeout). branches_for will need to take a &Connection. For commit, the fix as written opens a hole (see correction).

(a) Confirmed from the code. git.rs:666 is `e.register::<PrOpen>`, and lines 669-681 run `Command::new(gh).current_dir(&root).args(["pr","create"])` with `--fill` (or `--title`/`--body`), then `.output()`. Nothing bounds it with a timeout. For an ordinary `register` handler, dispatch takes `self.store.lock()` and opens the transaction at engine.rs:954-956, then calls the handler at 980. So gh runs from start to finish with the single store mutex held. This breaks D144 ("Every subprocess forked from a handler goes through proc::output_with_timeout"). D149's list of converted ops covers git.pr.list, git.push and git.fetch but not git.pr.open, and no DECISIONS entry exempts it. D148 even names "gh waiting on the network" as one of the things that used to hold this lock.

(b) Reachability. The user triggers it from the desktop "Open PR" button (code_git.rs:662). The button is enabled when the branch is pushed, has no commits ahead or behind, and has no PR yet (610-616), which is the normal "publish this session's work" step. The user then confirms a dialog (1240-1242) and the request goes over the main socket through guarded() and ui.call. `relay cmd` can trigger it too. Agents cannot reach it by default: the op is Actors::All, but no default role allowlist (settings.rs:36-38) includes it. It fires once for every PR opened from Relay.

(c) Consequence. gh pr create resolves the repo, looks for an existing PR and sends the createPullRequest mutation, which is several GitHub round trips. It also runs a local `git status`. That usually takes a few seconds, and tens of seconds to minutes on a bad network. For that whole time, every request that needs the store waits:
- every agent socket request, because check_session_token locks the store (engine.rs:1377-1378). This includes the PreToolUse hook's guardrail.gate call (relay-cli main.rs:465-471, with no client timeout), so every agent's Bash, Edit and Write call blocks, as do its MCP calls;
- all mutations;
- the ctx.read every unlocked query starts with;
- background system_write work;
- requests on the desktop's other connections, which fail with "Request timed out" after the client's 30 s limit (client.rs:65-71).
Keystrokes and terminal output are not affected: user session.input takes the D148 fast path, and resolve_actor returns None for Actor::User. After 30 s the Relay-installed PreToolUse hook timeout (hooks.rs:351) expires. I could not establish from this repo what Claude Code does then.

(d) High is right. Each use freezes the whole bus in a normal flow, which the rubric lists as high. It is not critical: the hold is bounded and short in the normal case, and only grows long on a degraded network.

(e) The proposed fix is correct in direction: register_staged, with a prepare that calls resolve_root_unlocked, gh_path, and output_with_timeout with GH_PROMPT_DISABLED=1, and a finish that only calls changed() and returns the URL. `emits` is not enforced (registry.rs:276), so keeping the event emit in finish is fine.

findings.evidence:
`e.register::<Dispatch>` (task.rs:1049) calls dispatch_task, which does `ctx.invoke_registered("session.create", ..)` (task.rs:683). For a staged op, invoke_registered runs the prepare phase against the already-open transaction (engine.rs:325-351). session.create's prepare (session.rs:857-893) does the following, all with the store mutex held:
- refresh_new_worktree -> fetch_remote (`git fetch --all --prune`, up to 20 s, git.rs:938-946);
- new_worktree_base;
- worktree::create (`git worktree add` with checkout and LFS smudge, up to 120 s, worktree.rs:212-215);
- the hook install.
With fanout:true this repeats sequentially for every child task (task.rs:1067-1075). The defect lives in task.rs/engine.rs; the slow calls are this chunk's fetch_remote and worktree::create.

CopyText returns `format!("#{} {}", task.id, task.title)` plus the body (1308-1316), and BUS.md calls it 'the copy button text, one place'. No Rust caller exists outside tests and perf. The native board builds `format!("{}

{}

#{id}", title, body)` on its own in two places (board_view.rs:1717, 2203).

