v2.reason:
(a) The code does what the finding says. In render_branch_picker, create_key's clicked closure owns `query = search.clone()` (code_git.rs:997-999), and search's activate closure owns `create = create_key.clone()` (1195-1196), so SearchEntry and create_key hold each other strongly. Search's search_changed and activate closures also own `entries`, which holds every row Box and key Button (1178-1179, 1194). create_key's closure owns `form` (996), which contains the create button. In the merge test, each CheckButton's toggled closure owns `test` (700-701) and test's clicked closure owns `picks` (719-727).

glib 0.22.9 `connect_raw` (glib-0.22.9/src/signal.rs:69-91) frees a Rust closure only through its destroy notify. That runs on disconnect or in g_object_real_dispose, so only after the last strong ref is gone. app.rs:64 `clear` only calls Box::remove, which unparents. No disconnect or run_dispose breaks either cycle.

The popover itself is freed: the rows and the create button hold it through `downgrade()`, and the MenuButton releases it when the header is disposed. Its show closure's ref to search therefore goes as well. Elsewhere the author uses weak refs for this same pattern: `submit = create.downgrade()` at 1010, and `row = key.downgrade()` in the scope picker in project_files.rs. So this is an oversight, and no DECISIONS entry covers it.

(b) The trigger is reachable in normal use, on the main page. `navigate("code")` is rewritten to page "agents" (app.rs:1314-1315), and that page holds the agent terminals. refresh_git runs:
- on every navigation there (app.rs:1351 -> prepare_project -> project_files.rs:66);
- after Git actions;
- from invalidate (editor.rs:508-528), coalesced to at most one per second. It has no check that the Git panel is visible, and the panel is hidden by default (editor.rs:300).

Every refresh that reaches line 779 with git.branches Ok leaks the picker group. create_key always exists, so this happens even with zero branches. The merge group leaks whenever the current project has at least one session that is not closed; ui.sessions is filtered to the current project (app.rs:1427-1431).

Refreshes are more frequent than the finding says. Watcher file.changed events go out through emit_system (watch.rs:128, engine.rs:567-569), and their envelope project_id is None. app.rs:1213 therefore does not drop them, so a change in any watched root of any project refreshes the current project. Watched roots include:
- the project root, once the project is opened;
- an agent worktree, once the user browses it or an agent calls file.read/tree/search or git.status.

Agent commits in any worktree also fire, because they write .git/refs/heads under the watched project root, and is_generated_path does not filter those writes. With agents editing in watched worktrees, refreshes approach one per second.

(c) Each refresh orphans:
- one SearchEntry, with its internal GtkText, IM context and icons;
- the create key and the form;
- every local branch row (21 here) and each remote-only key;
- each live session's CheckButton and the test button;
- their CSS nodes, gestures and closures.

That is a few hundred GObjects, roughly 100 KB per refresh in this repo. This is an estimate, not a measurement. It grows with branch count, and Relay adds a branch per session. The orphaned widgets are never drawn, so there is no CPU or visual effect. The client's memory (RSS) only grows until restart: a few MB per hour in light use, hundreds of MB per hour at the one-per-second ceiling. No data is lost, and the engine-owned sessions survive a client restart.

(d) This is unbounded growth in normal use, so it is high per the rubric. It is not critical: only the client's memory is affected, with no data loss and no security impact.

(e) The fix is correct.

